.TH KBD 2
.SH NAME
step,
is_modifier,
char_of,
rune_of,
extended_rune \- scancode translation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libkbd"
.PP
.nf
.B "Key :: distinct u16"
.B "State :: struct { shift, ctrl, alt, caps, extended: bool }"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "step :: proc(s: ^State, code: u8) -> (key: Key, down: bool, ok: bool)"
.B "is_modifier :: proc(key: Key) -> bool"
.B "char_of :: proc(s: ^State, key: Key) -> (r: rune, ok: bool)"
.B "rune_of :: proc(s: ^State, key: Key) -> (r: rune, ok: bool)"
.B "extended_rune :: proc(make: u8) -> (rune, bool)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Libkbd
is the scancode state machine both the kernel's keyboard driver and
.IR kbdfs (4)
translate PC scancode set 1 with, in a US layout.
It is the one copy of the translation both privilege levels call: the
kernel for
.BR /dev/cons ,
.I kbdfs
for the
.B cons
and
.B kbd
files it serves (see
.IR keyboard (6)).
.PP
A scancode names a
.I position
on the keyboard, a
.BR Key ,
not a character: what a position means depends on the modifier state
held at the moment.
.I Step
takes one raw scancode byte and updates
.IR s ,
answering the position it names and whether the key went down.
.B Ok
is false for the
.B 0xE0
extended prefix byte, which names no key on its own; the state
remembers it for the code that follows and sets the returned
.IR key 's
high bit.
Shift, control and alt move in
.I s
on both the press and the release, because their release matters.
Caps lock latches on the press only.
.PP
.I Is_modifier
answers whether a position is one whose only meaning is what it does to
the other keys: shift, control, alt, caps lock, num lock and scroll
lock.
.PP
.I Char_of
is what a cooked byte stream delivers for a press: the rune the position
means now, unless the key is a modifier or alt is held.
Both of those answer
.B ok
false, because a key pressed with alt held is a chord, and a chord makes
no character (see
.IR keyboard (6)).
.PP
.I Rune_of
is what a position means under the modifier state now, whether or not
alt is held.
A modifier key answers its own rune, so a reader of
.B kbd
sees it among the keys held.
A key behind the extended prefix answers
.IR extended_rune 's
answer.
A function key answers
.BR libkey.KF1
and up.
A position with a character on it answers the character, shifted,
cased by caps lock, and then made a control character by control, in
that order.
Control combined with anything but a letter answers
.B ok
false rather than a byte nobody meant.
Anything else, including a position this layout gives no meaning,
answers
.B ok
false.
.PP
.I Extended_rune
is the second half of an extended scancode as the rune Plan 9 gives that
key: the arrow keys, home, end, page up, page down, insert, delete and
print screen.
Keypad enter and the keypad slash are the two keys behind the prefix
that answer a character rather than a rune from
.IR libkey .
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libkbd/kbd.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR keyboard (6),
.IR kbdfs (4),
.IR edit (2)
.SH BUGS
Only scancode set 1 and a US layout are known.
There is no way to load another layout.
